## Waiting without burning a core

A thread often has to wait for another: for work to arrive, for a result, for initialization to finish.

```cpp
while (!ready) {}    // spins at 100% of a core, and is a data race on ready
```

This is **busy-waiting**. What is wanted is for the thread to sleep and be woken when the situation changes.

## `std::condition_variable`

A condition variable lets a thread sleep until another thread **notifies** it. It always works with a mutex protecting the state being waited on.

```cpp
#include <condition_variable>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>

std::mutex mutex{};
std::condition_variable workAvailable{};
std::queue<int> jobs{};

void producer()
{
    for (int i{ 0 }; i < 5; ++i)
    {
        {
            std::lock_guard lock{ mutex };
            jobs.push(i);
        }
        workAvailable.notify_one();
    }
}

void consumer()
{
    for (int n{ 0 }; n < 5; ++n)
    {
        std::unique_lock lock{ mutex };
        workAvailable.wait(lock, [] { return !jobs.empty(); });
        const int job{ jobs.front() };
        jobs.pop();
        lock.unlock();                 // do not hold the lock while working
        std::cout << "processing " << job << '\n';
    }
}

int main()
{
    std::jthread c{ consumer };
    std::jthread p{ producer };
    return 0;
}
```

`wait(lock, predicate)` does this, atomically where it matters:

1. If the predicate is already true, return at once.
2. Otherwise **release the lock and sleep**, as one indivisible step.
3. On waking, reacquire the lock and test the predicate again. If it is false, go back to step 2.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant P as Producer
    participant Q as queue and mutex
    participant C as Consumer
    C->>Q: lock, and find the queue empty
    Note over C: wait unlocks and sleeps
    P->>Q: lock, push a job, unlock
    P->>C: notify_one
    Note over C: wakes, relocks, rechecks the predicate
    C->>Q: pop the job, unlock
```

> [!important] Always wait with a predicate
> Two hazards make the bare `wait(lock)` wrong.
> **Spurious wake-ups:** a waiting thread may wake without any notification.
> **Lost wake-ups:** a notification sent before the thread starts waiting is not remembered.
> The predicate form handles both, because the condition is checked before sleeping and after every wake-up. The condition is the truth; the notification is only a hint to look again.

| Detail | Reason |
|---|---|
| `wait` needs a `std::unique_lock` | It must unlock and relock, which a `lock_guard` cannot do |
| Modify the shared state under the mutex | Otherwise a waiter can check, miss the change, and sleep for ever |
| Notify after releasing the lock | The woken thread can then acquire it immediately |
| `notify_one` or `notify_all` | One waiter can use the change; or all must re-examine it |

`std::condition_variable_any` accepts a `std::stop_token` in `wait`, so a `jthread` blocked in a wait can be cancelled.

## Semaphores

A **semaphore** holds a count of permits. `acquire()` takes one, blocking if none are left. `release()` returns one.

```cpp
#include <semaphore>

std::counting_semaphore<3> slots{ 3 };

void download(int id)
{
    slots.acquire();
    std::cout << "downloading " << id << '\n';
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds{ 1 });
    slots.release();
}
// started on ten threads, at most three download at any moment
```

```
 permits:  [ 1 ][ 2 ][ 3 ]       a fourth thread blocks until one is released
```

| | Mutex | Semaphore |
|---|---|---|
| Who releases | The thread that locked it | Any thread |
| Count | One holder | Up to N |
| Typical use | Protecting data | Limiting concurrency; signalling between threads |

`std::binary_semaphore` is a semaphore with one permit, often used as a light signal from one thread to another.

## `std::latch`

A **latch** is a counter that only goes down. Threads can block until it reaches zero. It is single-use.

```cpp
#include <latch>

std::latch ready{ 4 };
for (int i{ 0 }; i < 4; ++i)
{
    workers.emplace_back([&]
    {
        loadAssets();
        ready.count_down();
    });
}
ready.wait();    // continues only after all four have counted down
```

Use it for "wait until these N things have happened once", such as start-up.

## `std::barrier`

A **barrier** is a reusable meeting point for a fixed number of threads. Each calls `arrive_and_wait()` and blocks until all have arrived. Then an optional completion function runs once, and all are released for the next phase.

```cpp
#include <barrier>

std::barrier sync{ 4, []() noexcept { std::cout << "--- step complete ---\n"; } };

auto worker{ [&](int id)
{
    for (int step{ 0 }; step < 3; ++step)
    {
        simulatePart(id, step);
        sync.arrive_and_wait();
    }
} };
```

```
 step 0:   T0 ███    T1 █████    T2 ██    T3 ████
                                               │
                       all four have arrived ──┘
                       completion function runs once
 step 1:   all four start together
```

It suits simulations that advance in lock-step: every thread computes its part of generation N, and no one starts N+1 until all have finished.

## Which tool?

| You need to | Use |
|---|---|
| Protect shared data | `std::mutex` with a lock |
| Wait until a condition becomes true | `std::condition_variable` |
| Limit how many threads use something | `std::counting_semaphore` |
| Wait once for N events | `std::latch` |
| Run repeated phases in step | `std::barrier` |
| Receive one result from another thread | `std::future`: [[12.04 Futures, Promises, stdasync and Thread Pools]] |

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q{"What is being coordinated?"} -->|"access to data"| M["mutex"]
    Q -->|"a change of state"| CV["condition variable"]
    Q -->|"a number of slots"| S["semaphore"]
    Q -->|"a one-off set of events"| L["latch"]
    Q -->|"repeating phases"| B["barrier"]
    Q -->|"a single value"| F["future"]
```

## Check yourself
```quiz
? Why must `wait` be given a `std::unique_lock` and not a `std::lock_guard`?
- Because `std::lock_guard` cannot lock a mutex
+ `wait` must unlock and relock it, which `lock_guard` cannot
- Because `unique_lock` is faster
- Because `lock_guard` is deprecated
> `std::lock_guard` has no `unlock()`. A `std::unique_lock` can be unlocked and relocked by `wait`.

? What is the difference between a latch and a barrier?
- A latch is for threads; a barrier is for processes
- There is none
+ A latch counts down once and cannot be reused; a barrier resets after each phase
- A barrier counts down once; a latch resets after each phase
> Use a latch for "wait until N things are ready" once, and a barrier for threads that meet at the end of every round.

? Why always wait with a predicate?
- Because `wait` does not compile without one
- To make the wait time out
- To wake every waiting thread
+ Spurious wake-ups and early notifications are then harmless
> `cv.wait(lock, [&] { return ready; })` rechecks the real condition, so stray wake-ups and early notifications are harmless.

? How does a semaphore differ from a mutex?
+ It allows up to N holders, and any thread may release a permit
- It allows only one holder, like a mutex but faster
- It can only be used with condition variables
- It locks several mutexes at once
> A counting semaphore limits access to N, such as at most four downloads. A mutex has one owner who must unlock it.
```
